The TypeScript usage snippet imports `BasePresence` as a runtime value and calls `BasePresence.parse(...)`, but the runtime Zod schema is `BasePresenceSchema` (and `BasePresence` is a type alias). Update the snippet to import/use `BasePresenceSchema` for validation, and keep `BasePresence` as a type-only import.
```suggestion
import { BasePresenceSchema } from '@objectstack/spec/api';
import type { BasePresence, PresenceStatus, RealtimeRecordAction } from '@objectstack/spec/api';

// Validate data
const result = BasePresenceSchema.parse(data);
```

`resolvePluginName()` falls back to `manifest.name` / constructor name, but `pluginName` is later used as an ESM module specifier for dynamic `import()`. Human-readable names like "Marketplace Plugin" are not valid module IDs, so command loading will silently fail for common plugin objects (e.g., those imported via `os plugin add`). Consider storing an explicit `packageName`/`module` field in config entries, or deriving the import specifier from string plugin entries, and avoid using `manifest.name` as an import target.
```suggestion
 * Resolve a stable, machine-oriented plugin identifier.
 *
 * This value may be used as a package / module specifier, so it must
 * prefer explicit technical identifiers over human-readable labels.
 */
function resolvePluginName(plugin: Record<string, unknown>): string {
  // Prefer an explicit package or module identifier if present
  const pkgName = (plugin as { packageName?: unknown }).packageName;
  if (typeof pkgName === 'string' && pkgName.trim() !== '') {
    return pkgName;
  }

  const moduleId = (plugin as { module?: unknown }).module;
  if (typeof moduleId === 'string' && moduleId.trim() !== '') {
    return moduleId;
  }

  // Fallback to `name` only; avoid manifest names or constructor names,
  // which are typically human-readable and not valid module specifiers.
  if (typeof plugin.name === 'string' && plugin.name.trim() !== '') {
    return plugin.name;
  }
```

The TypeScript usage snippet imports `CLICommandContribution` / `CLIExtensionExport` as runtime values and calls `CLICommandContribution.parse(...)`, but only `CLICommandContributionSchema` / `CLIExtensionExportSchema` are runtime Zod schemas. Update the snippet to import and use the `*Schema` exports for `.parse(...)`, and import the non-`Schema` names as `type` only.
```suggestion
import { CLICommandContributionSchema, CLIExtensionExportSchema } from '@objectstack/spec/kernel';
import type { CLICommandContribution, CLIExtensionExport } from '@objectstack/spec/kernel';

// Validate data
const result = CLICommandContributionSchema.parse(data);
```

`loadPluginCommands` imports and registers commands once per *manifest entry*. If a plugin contributes multiple commands that point to the same module (or the module exports multiple commands), this loop will import the same module repeatedly and can register the same Command instances multiple times, leading to duplicate/ambiguous commands in Commander. Consider de-duping by moduleId (import once per module) and/or de-duping added commands by `cmd.name()` before calling `program.addCommand()`.
```suggestion
  const moduleCommandsCache = new Map<string, Command[]>();
  const registeredCommandNames = new Set<string>();

  // Load and register each contributed command
  for (const contribution of contributions) {
    try {
      const moduleKey = contribution.module ?? `${contribution.pluginName}:${contribution.name}`;

      let commands: Command[];
      if (moduleCommandsCache.has(moduleKey)) {
        commands = moduleCommandsCache.get(moduleKey)!;
      } else {
        commands = await importPluginCommands(contribution);
        moduleCommandsCache.set(moduleKey, commands);
      }

      for (const cmd of commands) {
        const commandName = cmd.name();
        if (registeredCommandNames.has(commandName)) {
          continue;
        }
        registeredCommandNames.add(commandName);
```

The TypeScript usage snippet imports `AriaProps` / `I18nLabel` / `I18nObject` as runtime values and calls `AriaProps.parse(...)`, but the runtime Zod schemas are `AriaPropsSchema`, `I18nLabelSchema`, and `I18nObjectSchema`. Update the snippet to use the `*Schema` exports for parsing, and keep the non-`Schema` names as type-only imports.
```suggestion
import { AriaPropsSchema, I18nLabelSchema, I18nObjectSchema } from '@objectstack/spec/ui';
import type { AriaProps, I18nLabel, I18nObject } from '@objectstack/spec/ui';

// Validate data
const result = AriaPropsSchema.parse(data);
```

The example code block has escaped braces (`\{` / `\}`) and extra blank lines, which makes the TypeScript example invalid/copy-paste unfriendly. Inside a fenced code block, these escapes aren’t necessary—please render it as valid TypeScript object literal syntax.
```suggestion
const label: I18nObject = {
  key: 'views.task_list.label',
  defaultValue: 'Task List',
  params: { count: 5 },
};
```
